<Doc
  title="AI Assist"
  description="Use your own ChatGPT plan, OpenAI API key or Grok account to write commit messages and pull requests."
  canonicalPath="/docs/ai-assist/"
>
  <p> AI Assist writes commit messages and pull request descriptions from what is on the machine: the staged patch, or the commits and diff a branch adds over its base. It uses a provider chosen by you, and Exeora runs no model of its own or bills you for model use. </p>

  <h2 id="linking">Choose a provider</h2>

  <p> <strong>ChatGPT plan.</strong> The local open-source path follows OpenAI's <a href="https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/" >Sign in with ChatGPT documentation</a >. The Exeora CLI on the machine that runs the request owns the registration and its owner-only credential files: </p>

  <pre>{`exeora chatgpt login
exeora chatgpt status
exeora chatgpt models
exeora chatgpt logout`}</pre>

  <p> An eligible ChatGPT Plus or Pro account can grant the <code>chatgpt.tokens.use.direct</code> permission for plan usage. A successful sign-in without that permission does not enable generations; run <code>exeora chatgpt login --enable-plan</code> to request it afterward. When it is enabled, the local CLI calls the public <a href="https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/models-and-inference" >OpenAI Responses API</a > at <code>https://api.openai.com/v1/responses</code>. The ChatGPT credential stays on that machine; it is not sent to the gateway, browser, D1 or Exeora Cloud. </p>

  <p> The sign-in callback is <code>http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/auth/callback</code>. Open the authorization page in a browser on the same machine as the CLI because <code>127.0.0.1</code> refers to the browser's computer. For a headless or SSH machine, run <code>exeora chatgpt login --no-browser</code> there and use its URL with a port-forward to that machine. ChatGPT plan usage is unavailable on Exeora Cloud machines. </p>

  <p> A ChatGPT plan request never silently falls back to an API key or another provider. Choose the OpenAI API provider explicitly when you want API-key billing. The official <a href="https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/sign-in" >registration and sign-in guide</a >, <a href="https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/profiles-and-sessions" >account and session guidance</a > and <a href="https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/preview-limitations" >preview limitations</a > describe the current provider contract. This page describes the local open-source path; it does not claim that the shared hosted gateway has enabled it or that live inference has been verified there. </p>

  <p> <strong>OpenAI API.</strong> Use an API key from the OpenAI platform when you want the existing gateway provider. It is separate from ChatGPT plan usage and remains available wherever the gateway offers AI Assist. </p>

  <p> <strong>Grok.</strong> Its subscription sign-in remains an unofficial xAI device flow for third-party applications and may change or close without notice. An xAI API key remains the documented alternative. A self-hosted gateway can offer API keys only. </p>

  <h2 id="settings">Provider, model and instructions</h2>

  <p>
    With more than one provider linked, one is the default and the button beside a field offers the
    other for that time. Each operation, commit messages and pull requests, can name its own provider
    and model, and carry instructions of its own: conventions to follow, a language to write in.
    They are added to what Exeora asks for, which is a short, imperative subject and a body that says
    why.
  </p>

  <h2 id="using">Using it</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Commit messages.</strong> The button beside the message in Source Control, once something is staged. The answer lands in the field only if the field is as it was when you asked; words you typed meanwhile stay, and a note says the generated text was set aside.</li>
    <li><strong>Pull requests.</strong> The button beside the title in the Pull Request view writes the title and the description from the commits and patch against the chosen base.</li>
    <li><strong>Ship changes.</strong> The whole trip from a working tree to an open pull request, described <a href="/docs/pull-request/#ship">with pull requests</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A generation can be stopped with the same button. It has a minute to answer; a provider that
    does not, or that refuses the token, is reported in words, and a token the provider no longer
    accepts asks you to link again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="old-links">Old ChatGPT subscription links</h2>

  <p> A subscription link created by Exeora's old unofficial Codex flow is a legacy link. It is not migrated or reused automatically. Remove it, then sign in again with <code>exeora chatgpt login</code> on the machine that will run the request, or choose an OpenAI API key. </p>

  <h2 id="data">What leaves the machine</h2>

  <p> The machine that holds the workspace reads the branch, staged files, commits and patch, then sends the bounded context through the relay to the gateway. The gateway constructs the generation prompt. For ChatGPT plan usage, it relays that prompt to the same machine, which calls the public Responses API with its local credential; the gateway never receives that credential. For an OpenAI API key or Grok provider, the gateway sends the prompt to the provider you chose. In every case, the audit stream records <code>ai.commit_message</code> or <code>ai.pull_request</code> with the project and workspace, never the content. The provider keeps what its own terms say it keeps. </p>
</Doc>
